  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper discusses grokking and compares it with its own chance-plateau/escape behavior, but its experiments do not measure delayed generalization after training-set memorization on the claim’s algorithmic datasets.",
    "gate_note": "The paper explicitly distinguishes its noisy associative-recall task from classic grokking: query accuracy remains near chance before escape, rather than measuring delayed test generalization after memorization. Its results concern a related plateau-to-escape behavior, so rows are recorded with versions [\"none\"].",
    "test_reason": "The study trains a ~78K-parameter mLSTM on MAD noisy recall, with fresh data every batch. It measures a chance-plateau followed by sharp escape in query accuracy, not delayed test generalization after training-set memorization. The authors say so explicitly in §4 and §12. It compares the shape to grokking and cites a grokking escape-law theory, but none of its results bear on any version as stated. There is no train/test split, no memorization phase, no algorithmic dataset, no Fourier circuit, no weight-decay efficiency test and no LLM setting.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that fixed-budget solved rate on MAD noisy recall is a censored measure of plateau-escape time and depends on schedule, learning rate and batch size. The orthogonalized read is a removable training scaffold, and failed models store about half the associations but fail at readout. They explicitly distinguish this from grokking, which is delayed generalization after memorization.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study trains a small mLSTM on MAD noisy associative recall and studies read-time orthogonalization and escape dynamics. It does not use the original grokking setup or measure train/test generalization on small algorithmic datasets.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Escape and solved fractions vary with learning-rate schedule, learning rate, batch size, vocabulary difficulty, and read orthogonalization.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "On noisy associative recall, the chance-level plateau and sharp escape are strongly sensitive to learning-rate schedule, learning rate and batch size. Escape hazard rises with per-step heat (+3.0) and falls with gradient noise lr²/B (−1.65).",
        "proposed_claim": "Plateau-then-escape transitions on small synthetic tasks (not post-memorization generalization) behave as noise-driven escape whose timing is set by schedule and gradient noise.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r3",
          "r5",
