          "r21"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "An orthogonalized read improves escape during training. Adding it at inference rescues no failed checkpoint, and it can be removed after escape with little loss.",
        "proposed_claim": "For this recall task, read orthogonalization is a removable training scaffold.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r2",
          "r4",
          "r22",
          "r23"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Behaviourally failed models (~3% accuracy) carry ~52% linearly decodable, key-specific associations; the wrong-key control gives ~5%.",
        "proposed_claim": "During an abrupt-transition plateau, task-relevant internal structure can be present before the behavioural metric moves. This is a hidden-progress analogue of V2, but on a different construct.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r24",
          "r25"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "n",
    "uncertainty_reported": "y",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/no-way-labs/recurrent-memory-scaffold",
    "peer_reviewed": "preprint",
    "problems": "The study uses one synthetic recall task and one small architecture; several comparisons use 8–16 seeds per cell. Its own text states that the findings do not establish transfer to other recall families or language-model scale. These experiments do not measure training-set memorization or delayed test generalization.",
    "rows_left_out": "Some reported ablation, memory-rank, gradient-coherence, and annealing results were omitted because they concern the proposed read mechanism or training scaffold rather than the claim’s delayed-generalization behavior. See §§5–6 and Tables 1–2.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No train vs test accuracy, no memorization phase, no training-set-size sweep (data are generated fresh each batch)."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "No generalizing circuit or memorizing component is tracked. The §9 storage/readout decode concerns recall associations, not a generalizing algorithm replacing memorization."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No image, text or molecule data. No initialization-scale or weight-norm switch of a grokking delay."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No LLM pretraining or fine-tuning."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "No weight-decay or low-norm manipulation, critical dataset size or ungrokking."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "No cross-run comparison of learned algorithms."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "Schedule, learning rate and batch size change plateau-escape timing on a recall task. The study does not test whether grokking's delayed generalization disappears when optimizer instabilities or softmax floating-point limits are controlled."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "All rows are correctly linked to none. Rows r2 and r4 and the ns5 rows compare the study's intervention with the baseline, which is fine as fits_no_version material. The analogy to grokking and the cited Ersoy and Wiesner escape law could interest V7 readers, but this task has no memorization phase, so nothing transfers as a test."
